This blog is a folder of markdown files and one build script. There is no
framework, no database, and nothing to install. Write a file, run the build, and
the site is on disk.

## Writing a post

Add a markdown file to `posts/`. The frontmatter block at the top is the only
required ceremony:

```
---
title: What I learned shipping alone
date: 2026-08-01
summary: One sentence for the index page and the RSS feed.
tags: [solo, shipping]
---

Your post starts here.
```

Only `title` and `date` really matter. Leave `summary` out and the first
paragraph is used instead. Set `draft: true` to keep a post out of the build
until it's ready — `npm run drafts` previews those locally.

The filename becomes the URL. Name it `what-i-learned.md` and it publishes to
`/posts/what-i-learned.html`. A date prefix works too: `2026-08-01-what-i-learned.md`
sorts nicely in your editor and publishes to the same clean URL.

## Formatting

The usual markdown works — **bold**, *italic*, `inline code`, [links](https://example.com),
and lists:

- Bullet lists
- Numbered lists
- Blockquotes and horizontal rules

Fenced code blocks keep their language label:

```js
export function hello(name) {
  return `hello, ${name}`;
}
```

> Posts are sorted newest first, and an RSS feed is generated at `/feed.xml`
> so readers can subscribe.

## Publishing

Run `npm run build` and everything lands in `public/` as plain HTML. That folder
is the whole site — host it on GitHub Pages, Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, S3, or
any box that can serve files.

Now delete this post and write your own.
